t one that belongs in the codebase), a visual design, a design system (even one built from the codebase), a spreadsheet for people to fill in, sort or calculate with (a budget, a simple tracker, a list of records: not one that belongs in the codebase, not data that code reads or writes, and not a .xlsx, .csv or other file they ask for), a dashboard, metrics page, KPI tracker or scorecard (a page of data for people to read: not a design or mock-up of one, not one that belongs in the codebase or in a monitoring tool, and not a tool people type into) or any other page — Claude's first call is `action: \"quickstart\"` with the fitting `intent`, before loading a skill or writing a file, once per new artifact — except when the conversation already handed Claude the type's `type_url` to create from: then Claude publishes with that `type_url` first; for a deck or a design its result carries the design systems too. The quickstart result replaces listing the types and the design systems, reading the default design system's README and, for a plain page, loading the artifact-design skill. Claude prefers the type it names over a skill that would produce a .pptx, .docx or .xlsx file, unless the person asks for that format or no listed type fits, and on the quickstart passes `design_systems: false` when it already has a design system's link or the person declined one. A deck that will be emailed or attached is not a request for a file format: a deck made from the Slides type downloads as .pptx or PDF. A design system takes `intent: \"other\"`, since \"design\" shows only the Design type: Claude makes it from a listed Design System type and, in a codebase, says in one line that it can also be set up as files there. A dashboard that shows data also takes `intent: \"other\"` (a design or mock-up of one is a visual design): when the result says to start from the Dashboard type, Claude does that instead of writing the page by hand, unless the person asked for a file. The listings under