ssion prompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If the user wants a different folder, tell them which computer and folder this session is connected to, and that they can disconnect it (how is below) and then ask you again. If the request is refused or the computer does not answer, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without it and say what remains blocked, including what the refusal says. You have no tool that disconnects the computer: only the user can. If they ask how: from this conversation's menu or its connected-computer indicator on claude.ai; or on that computer, wherever they set up Remote Control there (in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control; if the folder is served by the `claude remote-control` command, by stopping that command, which disconnects every session using the folder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder", "schema": {"description": "Connect THIS session to a folder the user's own computer serves through Remote Control, so this session's commands and file tools can run there. Calling this tool IS the request, and a person answers it every time (no permission mode and no stored rule does). So do NOT ask in chat first; once the task needs something that only exists on the user's computer, call list_computer_folders, then call this tool with the chosen folder's environment_id, computer_name and folder copied exactly — the request is refused if they do not match the folder's record. Only a folder under `folders` in that list can be requested; for one under `cannot_be_used`, give the user its explanation instead. The call waits for the computer. This can take a minute. The computer answers only while the Claude desktop app, or Claude Code's Remote Control, is running there. The result is final: `attached`, `refused` (the computer said no, and the result says why), or `did_not_answer` (no computer attached: it did not answer in time, or the user cancelled the request while it waited; nothing is left pending). A session is attached to at most one folder at a time, and it stays tied to the first computer it attaches to: to use a different computer, the user starts a new session. Asking again for the folder the session is attached to reconnects it: that attachment ends first, then the computer is asked afresh. While this session is attached to a folder (list_computer_folders shows it as `current`), do not call this tool for a different folder, and while an earlier request is still waiting, do not call it at all: the user would be shown the permission prompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If the user wants a different folder, tell them which computer and folder this session is connected to, and that they can disconnect it (how is below) and then ask you again. If the request is refused or the computer does not answer, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without it and say what remains blocked, including what the refusal says. You have no tool that disconnects the computer: only the user can. If they ask how: from this conversation's menu or its connected-computer indicator on claude.ai; or on that computer, wherever they set up Remote Control there (in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control; if the folder is served by the `claude remote-control` command, by stopping that command, which disconnects every session using the folder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"computer_name": {"description": "The folder's computer_name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}, "environment_id": {"description": "The folder's environment_id from list_computer_folders.", "type": "string"}, "folder": {"description": "The folder's name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["environment_id", "computer_name", "folder"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say so in your message, then call this tool, and do not propose a move for a credential again in this session. If the user declined the move, call this tool only if they then ask for the credential. You never receive the value — the result only says whether a credential is now configured, and if not, why. If the call itself is denied, because the user declined or because the app they are using cannot show the form, no result comes back: do not call this again for that host, and tell them the credential can be added in the environment's settings, then retry the failing request once it has been. After `saved`, retry the failing request right away. Until the proxy picks the credential up, the request can still fail with 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it expects for that command, never a real one: the proxy replaces it.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_credential", "schema": {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say so in your message, then call this tool, and do not propose a move for a credential again in this session. If the user declined the move, call this tool only if they then ask for the credential. You never receive the value — the result only says whether a credential is now configured, and if not, why. If the call itself is denied, because the user declined or because the app they are using cannot show the form, no result comes back: do not call this again for that host, and tell them the credential can be added in the environment's settings, then retry the failing request once it has been. After `saved`, retry the failing request right away. Until the proxy picks the credential up, the request can still fail with 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it expects for that command, never a real one: the proxy replaces it.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"display_name": {"description": "Optional short label to prefill in the form, e.g. \"Example API token\"; it names the credential in the environment's network secrets list. The user can change it. Plain text only: no URLs, links or markup.", "type": "string"}, "header_name": {"description": "Optional. The HTTP header the API reads the key from, e.g. \"xi-api-key\" or \"X-Api-Key\". Send it only when the user or the API's documentation says the key goes in a header other than Authorization: Bearer; leave it out otherwise. One word of letters, digits, hyphens and underscores: at most 40 characters, in at most 5 hyphen-separated parts of at most 16 characters each, and the last part must be key, apikey, token, auth, authorization, secret, credential or credentials. The form cannot save a key under any other header: for an API that reads one, the user adds the key in the environment's settings. Prefills the form; the user can change it. Not used with suggested_type \"basic\". Never put a key's value in this or any other field.", "type": "string"}, "host": {"description": "The exact hostname the credential is for, e.g. \"api.example.com\". Lowercase DNS name only — no scheme, path, port, IP address or wildcard. Shown to the user in the form.", "type": "string"}, "prefix": {"description": "Optional. The word the API expects before the key in the header: one of Bearer, Token, Key, ApiKey, Api-Key, Bot, SSWS, Splunk or GenieKey, in any letter case. With the Authorization header it defaults to Bearer; with any other header_name it defaults to none, so pass prefix whenever the documentation shows a word before the key, Bearer included. The form cannot save a key with any other word before it: for an API that expects one, the user adds the key in the environment's settings. Not used with suggested_type \"basic\". Never put a key's value in this or any other field.", "type": "string"}, "reason": {"description": "One or two sentences: which request failed or which step needs the credential, and what it unblocks. The form may not show this, so say in your message, before you call, which network secret you need, for which host, and why. The secret's value is entered only in the form, never in chat. Plain text only: no URLs, links or markup (name the host instead).", "type": "string"}, "suggested_type": {"description": "Optional hint for the form's default: \"bearer\" for a token or API key sent in a header (Authorization: Bearer unless header_name and prefix say otherwise), \"basic\" for username and password.", "enum": ["bearer", "basic"], "type": "string"}}, "required": ["host", "reason"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_credential"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Schedule a message to be delivered back into THIS SESSION at a future time. Use it to remind yourself to resume work, check on something, or continue after a delay. If a turn is running in this session when the message is due, the message waits and is normally delivered after that turn ends. It does not interrupt a running turn. Delivery survives container restarts. Granularity is one minute — the scheduler polls every minute, so sub-minute precision is not available. This is a thin wrapper over create_trigger (a one-shot scheduled task bound to this session); the returned trigger_id can be passed to delete_trigger to cancel before it fires, and the scheduled task disables itself after firing once. When telling the user what you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_later", "schema": {"description": "Schedule a message to be delivered back into THIS SESSION at a future time. Use it to remind yourself to resume work, check on something, or continue after 